<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · island</p><h1 class="enc-title">Maupiti</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Maupiti is the westernmost high island of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/leeward-islands-society-islands">Leeward Society Islands</a> in French <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/polynesia">Polynesia</a>, a small volcanic island surrounded by a lagoon and low coral islets, about 40 kilometres west of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bora-bora">Bora Bora</a>. In the 18th century it was conquered by the chiefs of Bora Bora. It has notable marae and early archaeological sites that have shed light on the settlement of eastern Polynesia.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/maupiti.jpg" alt="Maupiti" width="320" height="320" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: NASA · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Maupiti_ISS002.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
